===Etymology===
{{attention|en|What is "Old Low Frankish" supposed to be referring to?}}
{{inh+|en|enm|orgulous}}, {{m|enm|orgeilous}}, {{der+|en|fro|orgueilleus}}, {{m|fro|orgoillus||proud}}, from {{m|fro|orgoill}}, {{m|fro|orgueil||pride}}, from Old Low {{der|en|frk|*urgol||pride}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|urguol||excellent}}, {{cog|ang|orgel||pride}}, perhaps from a {{der|en|gem-pro|*uzgōljō}}, equivalent to {{af|en|or-|t1=out}} {{m|ang|*gōl|t=boast; showiness; pomp; splendor}}, related to {{cog|ang|galan||to sing}} (whence Modern English {{m|en|gale}}). Also perhaps partly from {{der|en|fro|orgoill}}, {{der+|en|VL.|*orgōllia}}, {{m|VL.|*orgōlla}}, {{der+|en|frk|*uʀgōllju|*orgōllja}}, from the same {{der|en|gem-pro|-}} source. Cognate with {{cog|goh|urguol}}, {{m|goh|urguoli}}, {{m|goh|urgilo||pride}} and {{cog|es|orgullo}}.

