===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[proud|Proud]]; [[haughty]]; [[disdainful]].
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|page=52|book=II|chapter=IV|text=At that time there was a knight, the which was the king's son of Ireland, and his name was Lanceor, the which was an '''orgulous''' knight, and counted himself one of the best of the court; and he had great despite at Balin for the achieving of the sword, that any should be accounted more hardy, or of more prowess.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses|14|text=Then spoke young Stephen '''orgulous''' of mother Church that would cast him out of her bosom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1966|author=Eric Walter White|title=Stravinsky the Composer and his Works|publisher=University of California Press|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=rZVRysD6FJsC&pg=PA5|page=5|text=Her nephew describes her as 'an '''orgulous''' and despotic woman', and it is clear that he noticed and resented her numerous unkindnesses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|author=w:Georgette Heyer|title=w:My Lord John|publisher=Arrow Books|year_published=2011|isbn=0099476428|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=JYSDcytAHRwC&pg=PA14|page=14–15|text=They knew that my lord of Arundel had grown so '''orgulous''' that he had lately dared to marry the Earl of March's sister, without license.}}
# [[ostentatious|Ostentatious]]; [[showy]].
# [[swollen|Swollen]]; [[augmented]]; [[excessive]].
#* '''1967''', {{w|John T. Sladek}}, ''Masterson and the Clerks,'' reprinted in ''{{w|Michael Moorcock bibliography|Best SF Stories from New Worlds 4}},'' edited by Michael Moorcock
#*: {{quote|en|The smile became an orange balloon, '''orgulous''' and threatening.}}
# [[threatening|Threatening]]; [[dangerous]].

